WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.340
- This is the Ruhr Valley in Germany.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.040
And it used to be a mining region.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.620
It used to produce huge
amounts of anthracite,

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:09.540
the sort of coal that you have to dig
deep, deep tunnels to reach.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.410
Sometimes more than a
kilometre down into the ground.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:16.070
But the last deep coal mine
here closed at the end of 2018

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:18.670
as the world steadily
shifts to renewables.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:23.220
But you can see the legacy of that mining
in the landscape, in places like this.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.860
Strange hills which are old spoil tips.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.130
Dumping sites for everything that
was dug up, that wasn't coal.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:33.580
All the earth and rock that was moved
out of the way to create the mine shafts.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.520
They're steadily being reclaimed.
Trees have been planted all over.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.150
And several of them now have
art like this at the top.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.580
I visited one art piece called
the Tetrahedron yesterday.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.530
The weather was bad and there was fog.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:47.390
And honestly it felt like I was on

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.280
some sort of alien planet
from a sci-fi show.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.890
The hill I'm standing on
has an amphitheatre as well,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.340
although it's a little rundown now.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.640
It is very, very difficult to get across
the sheer scale of these on camera,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:04.610
the incredible volume of earth and
rock that was moved over decades.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.980
Don't get me wrong, the Ruhr Valley
is still industrial.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.910
There are still opencast coal mines

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:10.540
and factories producing
all sorts of things.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.930
But it does seem like there's more and more
talk about the legacy of industry here

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.760
than actual industry itself.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.623
One old coal mine is now a
museum and World Heritage Site.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.940
There's another strange legacy
of the old coal mines here, though.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:30.100
If you take that much material out from
under the surface and put it on top,

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.830
the mass of it starts
changing the landscape

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:34.030
in a very different way.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:39.870
Parts of this valley sank unevenly,
as far as 20 metres down in places.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.980
And that altered the way
that rainwater drains.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.800
In that: it starts to drain
towards where people live.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.820
So yesterday, I got to visit
one of the pumping stations

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.840
that puts the water back
where it's supposed to be.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.340
- The problem is that we have coal mining
in this area since 1850.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.270
And because of this, the
landscape has sunk down.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:06.400
And so we had to pump the whole River Boye
up 18 metres to the river Emscher.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.220
And the river Emscher
is flowing to the Rhine.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:12.544
In my operational area, we
have about 60 pump stations,

00:02:12.544 --> 00:02:15.940
from a very small station
with a few hundred litres,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.000
to this station with
40,000 litres per second.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.793
83 square kilometres is our
operational area, this one station.

00:02:23.793 --> 00:02:27.530
From my operational area,
nearly 60% would be flooded

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.540
if the whole pump stations wouldn't work.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.560
If you have dry conditions,
it would be four to five days.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.620
In heavy rain conditions only five hours.
The water will get out.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.520
- Just to be clear, those
pumps are moving a whole river,

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.740
a small river, but a whole river,
18 metres up

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.240
and through massive underground pipes.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.110
So the water can then join a bigger river
that does eventually flow to the sea.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:55.380
There are around 180 pumping stations,
large and small, across this valley.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.910
That one station is the largest.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:02.920
After a heavy storm, it will move up to
40,000 litres of water every second.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.050
If you look at articles about this,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.930
they'll sometimes mention
groundwater and aquifers.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.130
But the engineers here
told me that is wrong.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:10.750
It is probably a mistranslation.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:13.610
These are storm drains
to deal with rainwater.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.540
- The coal mining companies
are paying for this

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.060
because they had dug the coal.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.699
And because of this, the
landscape has sunk down.

00:03:20.699 --> 00:03:22.320
So they have to pay for.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.750
- When coal mining started here
150 years ago,

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.370
no one thought about the land sinking.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.380
And when they did notice it,
which was somewhere around 1900,

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:34.010
it didn't stop the mining.
They just built the first pumps.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:37.940
The coal was worth the
money and the trouble then.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:40.460
But running the pumps is an eternal cost.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.170
Not technically eternal, I guess.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:46.750
But this won't go away in 100,
or 1,000, or 10,000 years.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:51.750
Not until something equally dramatic and
geological comes along to reshape the land.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.070
If people want to live in the Ruhr Valley,

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.670
not the Ruhr Swamp or the Ruhr Lake,

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.403
then the pumps must run.

